/*!
    \page working-with-server.html
    \title Working with a Server
    \previouspage OPC UA Server
    \nextpage Configuration

    This chapter describes the common end-to-end scenarios that combine the client and
    the server halves of the application.

    \section1 Design, run, and test a server locally

    \list 1
        \li In Server Studio, design an address space and set an endpoint and security
            (see \l {OPC UA Server}).
        \li Start the server. The runtime process starts and reports its endpoint.
        \li Use \e{Open in Client} to connect the built-in client to the running server;
            the client connects to the local endpoint anonymously.
        \li Browse the server, read and write variables, and subscribe to values
            (see \l {OPC UA Client}).
    \endlist

    Because the server runs in its own process, you can stop, restart, or kill it while
    the client stays open — a realistic way to test client reconnect behavior.

    \section1 Test security and identities

    Configure the server with signing and encryption and with username/password users,
    then connect the client using the matching security policy and identity. This
    exercises certificate exchange (using each side's PKI store) and access control end
    to end.

    \section1 Simulate live data

    Attach simulations to variables (counter, sine, ramp, and so on) so the server emits
    changing values. Subscribe to those variables in the client and watch the
    \e{Data Access} view and trend panel update from real data-change notifications.

    \section1 Clone an existing server

    Connect the client to a third-party server, browse the part of the address space you
    care about, and clone it into a new server project. You then have an editable
    \c .uaserver reproduction you can run locally, secure, and simulate.

    \section1 Exchange address spaces

    Use NodeSet2 import/export to move an address space between this tool and other OPC
    UA tooling, or to keep a versioned copy of a designed model.
*/
